module Auth.Simple.Credential exposing ( Credential, Validity(..) )

{-| The generic credential abstraction: a secret a user proves they hold (a
password, a one-time code, ...). This core is exactly what authentication
consumes — [`Auth.Simple.Login`](Auth.Simple.Login) drives `validate` and
`burn` and nothing else, so every kind of credential offers one of these and
one `login` function covers them all.

How a credential gets *into* the database is kind-specific and lives beside
this core: a durable secret prepared by the caller is a
[`Stored`](Auth.Simple.Credential.Stored) credential
([`Password`](Auth.Simple.Credential.Password) exposes `credential` and
`stored`); a short-lived secret is minted by its own module and delivered out
of band ([`Code`](Auth.Simple.Credential.Code) exposes `credential` and
`request`).

@docs Credential

@docs Validity

-}

import Crypto
import Sqlite
import Task exposing ( Task )


{-| The outcome of validating a candidate secret. An expected negative —
the secret doesn't match (`Invalid`), or the credential is temporarily locked
after repeated failures (`Locked`) — is a value, not an error; the `error` type
of a [`Credential`](#Credential) is reserved for real faults (database, crypto).
This mirrors `Token.verify` returning a `Maybe`.
-}
type Validity
    = Valid
    | Invalid
    | Locked


{-| The two operations every credential supports: validate a candidate secret
against whatever is on record for the user, and burn the work of a validation
without one.

A `Credential` is a pure bundle of functions — each one is handed the
`Sqlite.Database` and `Crypto.SecureContext` per call (by the workflow that
drives it, e.g. [`Auth.Simple.Login`](Auth.Simple.Login)), so it holds no
config of its own. `candidate` is the secret type the user presents at login;
`error` is the failure type for real faults.

`burn` exists for timing equalization: it must cost the same work as `validate`
against a dummy stored secret and never fail, so a workflow can run it when no
user or secret exists and then fail uniformly — keeping "unknown identifier"
indistinguishable from "wrong secret" to a clock-watching attacker. A credential
whose validation is cheap may implement it as cheaply.
-}
type alias Credential error candidate =
    { validate : -- Given a candidate secret, validate it against the stored one
        { userId : String
        , secret : candidate
        }
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error Validity
    , burn : -- Do the work of a validation without a stored secret; never fails
        candidate
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error {}
    }
